 add('## Replay method and limits')
 add('The engine began from the preceding real terminal board, previous battle outcome, persistent shop buffs and frozen stock. The parser preserved five physical board positions, with x=4 at the front. Absent Enu and x decode to zero. Permanent and temporary stats remain separate. Each subsequent purchase, merge, sale, food, roll, freeze and end-of-turn trigger runs through native code. The shim binds observed species or food variants to offer IDs at generation. Prior frozen items are hydrated with their real stats and cost. Newly generated named items start with native base stats and receive native modifiers. UID labels are scoped to a seat. A complete input-side freeze inventory can establish the relative positions of otherwise opaque offers. Unknown rolled-away offers can occupy inert slots; they never serve as a purchase of a guessed species. Eviction and rare extra generation remain possible sources of UID divergence. [Replay implementation](replay.py) · [Identity ledger](identity.py)')
 add('Type 4 starts the shop, 5 rolls, 6 pays for a shop pet at a square (including buy-merge), 7 combines source onto target, 8 buys the food named in the response, 9 sells, 11 ends the turn, and 12 names the team. Type 0 supplies Build and Battle. The checked state from Build.Bor agreed with the same seat’s pre-battle UserBoard for board, shop, allocation counter and money on all 8,121 snapshots. Type 1 carries mode/opponent updates, type 2 is bookkeeping, and nine type 3 records contained no shop request. No free Type 6 board move or paid Type 7 was needed to reconcile this corpus. Real board reorders come through §Request.Data.BoardOrders§; freeze flags come through §BoardFreezes§ on several different action types. Two explicit merges after a Pill use a newly summoned board source. Empty board purchases and shifting a different species toward a vacancy were distinguished from buying a copy onto the resident.')
 add('A pet seen in a later snapshot supplies its species as a replay input. Direct explicit merges also establish equal species. For an unnamed purchase onto an occupied square, the decoder can infer absorption when the source has no later board reference, is absent from the ending board, and no later Pill cascade could have destroyed it. It does not read the final pet stats to make that inference. Without any of these implicit source inferences the score was 4,778/4,891 (97.69%). On turns with no pet purchase at all it was 721/735 (98.10%). These are conditional action-replay scores. The experiment does not regenerate the original shop inventory from a real-game seed. [Extraction](extract.py) · [Coverage checks](SUMMARY.json)')
